SHAMSU — the web portal: the link, the black hole, the disconnection
====================================================================
2026-08-23 · branch small-shamsu-new

Reported: "the web is not working as it should, the chat is all crooked and out
of sync, and when i give a prompt from the web the thing didnt send the cli or
the system, and had an error. also we dont have to verify the web session with
tokens, it should be directly accessible with the simple link."

Diagnosed by driving the portal exactly as a browser does, against the real
session `20260822-183305-4975`:

    0 user      turn-775 src=cli | Act as an expert game developer. Scaffold...
    1 assistant turn-775 src=cli | The model did not respond within 600s.
    2 user      turn-fe7 src=web | okay can you wirte the plan in an md file?
    3 user      turn-a26 src=web | say hi

Two web turns, both accepted, NEITHER ANSWERED. That is the report, in the data.

ONE THING I SUSPECTED AND WAS WRONG ABOUT: I thought the message ordering was
scrambled, because the tail of the list read assistant-then-user-then-user. It
is correct - turn 775 timed out, and the two web turns simply never produced an
answer to interleave. Checked before saying so.


PHASE 1 — the link is the whole thing
--------------------------------------
The shell (`GET /`) was served WITHOUT a token, because it has to be: the
browser has nowhere to put one before the page loads. Every `/api/*` demanded
one. So `http://127.0.0.1:8765/` - the address anyone would type or bookmark -
rendered a complete-looking application that then failed every single request
with a 401, silently. A link that half-works is worse than one that refuses.

`WebPortal.requires_token` is now False on a loopback bind, and `url` is the
bare address.

WHAT STILL GUARDS IT: the portal binds 127.0.0.1 and is unreachable from
anywhere else on the network, and `_origin_is_local` refuses a foreign Origin -
the DNS-rebinding attack the token was never the answer to. Both were already
there and both were doing the actual work.

WHAT IT DOES NOT GUARD, stated plainly: another program on this machine can now
drive the agent, which writes files and runs commands. That is the trade.

The token comes straight back when the bind is not loopback, because at that
point anything on the LAN could do the same. `SHAMSU_WEB_TOKEN=1` demands it
anyway.

Also corrected while in there: three panels described the portal as
"read-only ... cannot start or stop a run". It was never read-only -
`POST .../prompt` has always started a turn - and saying otherwise made it look
safer than it is.


PHASE 2 — a prompt cannot vanish
---------------------------------
`_looks_like_id` only rejects path-shaped strings:

    'None'      -> True
    'undefined' -> True

So `POST /api/workspaces/{id}/sessions/undefined/prompt` returned
**202 accepted, queue_id 3**, and the turn then ran against a thread nobody
could open: no answer, no error, no trace. Demonstrated, not theorised.

`api.session_exists` now gates the route; an unknown id is a 404 where it is
sent.

The second half: a worker that raised marked its queue row CANCELLED and said
nothing else. Surfaces render from the turn stream, so the browser saw
`turn.start` and then silence for ever - a bubble that never resolves, which is
exactly what "out of sync" looks like from the outside.
`QueuedRunner._announce_failure` publishes `error` + `turn.end status=failed`,
so a turn that dies ends as visibly as one that succeeds.


PHASE 3 — the surfaces were not out of sync, they were not connected
---------------------------------------------------------------------
The important one, and the answer to "it didn't send to the cli".

`WebPortal.runner` builds its own `QueuedRunner(surface="web")`, which
constructs a SEPARATE `SimpleChatLoop` with a SEPARATE `TurnStream`. A renderer
is attached to one stream by whoever built it - so the CLI, which only ever
attached to streams it built itself, had no way to know the web's existed. A
prompt sent from the browser ran to completion in the same process with the
terminal showing nothing at all.

`TurnStream.add_observer` watches EVERY stream in this process. Process-wide
rather than passed down, because the alternative is threading a display through
`QueuedRunner`, the Telegram bridge and every future entry point, each of which
would then have to remember.

The framed TUI registers one and now shows, in the surface's own colour:

    ◈ write the plan to an md file        <- a prompt from the browser
    ◆ Wrote PLAN.md with 5 steps.         <- its answer
    · web turn failed                     <- or how it died

Deliberately NOT the tool rows: a foreign turn's forty action lines interleaved
with this terminal's own would make both unreadable, and the browser is already
showing them to whoever is watching there.


PHASE 4 — the browser stops flying blind
-----------------------------------------
`case "status": setFooter(event.text)` - the text was rendered and `event.data`
was dropped on the floor. Every number was already riding in on the event and
none were shown, so a run 19 rounds deep at 84% of its window with the model at
3 tok/s looked exactly like a healthy one until it failed.

    thinking... 50s · rnd 14/24 · ctx 71% · 34 tok/s

And `turn.end` now colours the footer red when the verdict is `failed`, because
a turn that died must not read as one that worked.


TESTS
-----
    tests/test_web_access.py    12 passed  (new)
    tests/test_webui.py         ...
    tests/test_web_conversation.py, test_web_in_loop.py, test_webui_settings.py
                               116 passed combined
    tests/test_tui.py          131 passed with test_turn_stream_parity.py

Four existing tests in `test_webui.py` asserted the OLD token behaviour and now
assert the policy instead - a loopback request is served, and `SHAMSU_WEB_TOKEN=1`
brings the 401 back. Two more asserted the "read-only" wording that was never
true. Changed deliberately, and each says so in place.

Worth naming:

    test_the_plain_link_drives_the_whole_api
    test_a_bind_beyond_this_machine_still_demands_the_token
    test_a_foreign_origin_is_still_refused
        the DNS-rebinding defence is what actually guards a loopback portal and
        must not have gone away with the token.
    test_a_prompt_for_a_session_that_does_not_exist_is_refused
        parametrised over `undefined`, `None` and a well-formed id that is not
        there.
    test_a_failed_turn_ends_visibly_on_the_stream
    test_a_turn_the_terminal_never_started_still_shows_up
        publishes on a stream the CLI never built, asserts the prompt, the
        answer and the failure all land - then detaches and asserts silence.
    test_a_foreign_turns_tool_rows_do_not_flood_this_terminal
    test_an_observer_that_raises_never_fails_the_turn


NOT DONE
--------
The 600s timeout itself. Both web turns in the reported session died on it, and
that is a model/context problem rather than a portal one - the portal's fault
was only that it never said so. Phases 2 and 4 make it visible; making it
rarer is Fix 08 territory.
